= 1
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Take $\alpha>0$, $t\ne0$, and the negative-norm convention for a <timelike curve> used by the printed metric. Writing $v=dx/dt$ and $ds^2=-c^2d\tau^2$ gives
$$
\frac{d\tau}{dt}=\frac{|t|}{\sqrt\alpha}\sqrt{1-\frac{v^2}{c^2}}.
$$
Spatial translation is a <Killing vector field>. Equivalently, the <geodesic Lagrangian> has the <cyclic coordinate> $x$, so its conserved momentum is
$$
P=\frac{t^2}{\alpha}\frac{dx}{d\tau}
=\frac{|t|}{\sqrt\alpha}\frac{v}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}.
$$
Therefore the correct <first integral> for this metric is
$$
\boxed{\frac{|t|\,v}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}=\text{constant}.}
$$
**The first-integral assertion in the PDF is false.** Its dimensionless square root requires $v/c$, and even after setting $c=1$ it is missing the factor $|t|$. For example, the nonvertical geodesics below have $v/\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}$ proportional to $1/|t|$.

Set $b=\sqrt\alpha P/c$. Solving for the velocity, with increasing $t$ as the time orientation, gives $v=cb/\sqrt{t^2+b^2}$. The complete unparametrized family of <timelike geodesics of a quadratic-time conformal metric> is
$$
\boxed{x(t)=x_c+cb\,\operatorname{arsinh}\!\left(\frac{t}{|b|}\right)\quad(b\ne0),\qquad
x(t)=x_c\quad(b=0).}
$$
Each curve is restricted to a connected positive- or negative-$t$ patch; $t=0$ is excluded because the metric becomes degenerate. For $t\ne0$, $|v|<c$, and the two independent constants are $x_c$ and $b$. If an explicit affine parametrization is desired,
$$
\tau-\tau_c=\frac{t\sqrt{t^2+b^2}-b^2\operatorname{arsinh}(t/|b|)}{2\sqrt\alpha}\quad(b\ne0),
$$
while the vertical family has $d\tau=|t|dt/\sqrt\alpha$. A reversal of the time orientation reverses the parameter without changing these paths.

= 2
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Put $\kappa=8\pi G/c^4$ and $T=T_\nu{}^\nu$. The <contracted Bianchi identity> and <metric compatibility> give
$$
\left(\frac12-\alpha\right)\partial_\mu R
=-\kappa\nabla_\nu T_\mu{}^\nu.
$$
Taking the <trace> of the field equation in four dimensions gives $(1-4\alpha)R=-\kappa T$. For $\alpha\ne1/4$, eliminate $R$:
$$
\boxed{\nabla_\nu T_\mu{}^\nu
=\frac{\tfrac12-\alpha}{1-4\alpha}\,\partial_\mu T.}
$$
Ordinary covariantly coupled matter obeys <stress-energy conservation>, $\nabla_\nu T_\mu{}^\nu=0$. Its <trace> need not be constant, so the <trace coefficient fixed by stress-energy conservation> is
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\frac12.}
$$
This restores the <Einstein tensor> and is the only coefficient compatible with arbitrary conventional matter without adding a new constraint or changing the matter conservation law. Other coefficients can work for specially restricted sources with constant <trace>, but do not give the same universal matter coupling.

The excluded case $\alpha=1/4$ must be treated before dividing: its <trace> equation requires $T=0$. If stress-energy is also conserved, its divergence equation requires $\partial_\mu R=0$. It can describe a restricted traceless-source sector with constant scalar curvature, not general matter such as ordinary dust. In particular it is not the general <trace>-free Einstein equation with source $T_{\mu\nu}-g_{\mu\nu}T/4$, which is a different equation.

= 3
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Interpret the orbit in the <Schwarzschild metric> as an unforced circular <timelike geodesic> and choose its plane as $\theta=\pi/2$. Put $f=1-2\mu/r$. The radial <geodesic equation> at constant radius uses the <Christoffel symbols>
$$
\Gamma^r{}_{tt}=\frac{c^2\mu f}{r^2},\qquad
\Gamma^r{}_{\phi\phi}=-fr,
$$
and gives the <angular velocity of a circular Schwarzschild geodesic>
$$
\Omega^2=\left(\frac{d\phi}{dt}\right)^2=\frac{c^2\mu}{r^3}=\frac{GM}{r^3}.
$$
The <proper-time normalization> then yields
$$
\left(\frac{d\tau}{dt}\right)^2=f-\frac{r^2\Omega^2}{c^2}=1-\frac{3\mu}{r}.
$$
Over one revolution, the <proper period of a circular Schwarzschild geodesic> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\Delta\tau=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^3}{GM}}\sqrt{1-\frac{3GM}{c^2r}},\qquad r>3GM/c^2.}
$$
Both the gravitational and orbital kinematic contributions to <time dilation> are included. Circular timelike geodesics exist for $r>3\mu$; they are stable for $r>6\mu$ and unstable for $3\mu<r<6\mu$, with marginal stability at $6\mu$.

If “circular orbit” instead means a forced trajectory with an externally chosen <angular velocity>, the radius alone does not fix the answer. Its elapsed time is $\Delta\tau=\Delta t\sqrt{1-2\mu/r-r^2\Omega^2/c^2}$, or $2\pi\sqrt{1-2\mu/r-r^2\Omega^2/c^2}/|\Omega|$ per revolution. The <geodesic> interpretation supplies the missing <angular velocity>.

